🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝘂𝗱𝗱𝗲𝗻𝗹𝘆 𝗦𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗖𝗣𝗨 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝗿𝗺𝗮𝗹 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes application has suddenly become slow, but CPU and memory usage look normal. How will you troubleshoot it?”

This is where I would look beyond pod resources.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for:

🔹 Timeouts
🔹 Connection errors
🔹 Slow requests
🔹 Retries
🔹 Dependency failures

2️⃣ Check the Service

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl get endpoints -n <namespace>

Make sure traffic is reaching the expected pods.

3️⃣ Check network connectivity

Test communication between the application and its dependencies:

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- curl <service>:<port>

Check for DNS or network-related delays.

4️⃣ Check dependencies

If the application itself looks healthy, investigate:

🔹 Database latency
🔹 External APIs
🔹 Cache
🔹 Other microservices
🔹 Network latency

5️⃣ Check recent changes

Look for:

🔹 Recent deployment
🔹 Configuration changes
🔹 Database changes
🔹 Increased traffic
🔹 Infrastructure/network changes

6️⃣ Compare with the baseline

I would compare current response times with normal application metrics.

The goal is to identify when the latency started and what changed around that time.

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

User → Ingress/LB → Service → Pod → Application → Database/Dependencies

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Normal CPU + Memory does NOT always mean a healthy application.

Sometimes the bottleneck is outside the pod — especially database, network, external APIs, or another microservice.
